;; DRIVER-LL Phase 4, sub-phase 4c: the content-shape validation channel.
;;
;; validate.llmll:56-63 reserved this module by name. Its verdict-of decides
;; PRESENCE and a declared byte FLOOR, and it takes no string, which is the
;; property that keeps a subject's conventions out of the decision. The three
;; content-shape validators of rfc_to_implementation.py -- check_extraction
;; (:386, stage D), the core check (:713-714, stage F) and check_dispositioned
;; (:481, stage G) -- decide over the CONTENT of a delegated artifact, so they
;; cannot be a widening of verdict-of. They are a second channel, and this is
;; it.
;;
;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;; 1. WHY EVERY PARAMETER HERE IS A bool OR AN int.
;;
;;    The obvious shape for a content validator takes the artifact. That shape
;;    is wrong twice over, and the second reason was measured rather than
;;    reasoned:
;;
;;    SUBJECT-NEUTRALITY. Section 7:288-291 forbids a validator that hardcodes
;;    one run's conventions. verdict-of earns that structurally by taking no
;;    string. A content validator taking Json would forfeit it, because every
;;    row of every census would be in scope and nothing but review would stop a
;;    body from reading one.
;;
;;    FRAGMENT PRESERVATION. `regex-match` is a compiler builtin
;;    (TypeCheck.hs:143, Syntax.hs:502), so `^C[1-6]$` (:490) ports verbatim.
;;
;;    CORRECTED 2026-08-10 by TOOL-RFC-005, which measured it. This comment
;;    said `^N\d+$` (:422) ports verbatim too. IT DOES NOT. `regex-match`
;;    lowers to Text.Regex.TDFA (CodegenHs.hs, `subject =~ pattern`), which is
;;    POSIX ERE and has no `\d`: executed against TDFA, `^N\d+$` does not match
;;    "N123", while `^N[0-9]+$` does. The claim was a feasibility read written
;;    next to the code and never run. TDFA does support `\b`, which the same
;;    probe confirmed, so the dialect narrows in one direction only.
;;
;;    But LLMLL.md:326 puts regex-match in the boolean-builtin class, "never
;;    auto", and LLMLL.md:356 states the consequence: a predicate outside
;;    Sigma_auto still erases, but the carrying function falls to
;;    erBodyFallback and the body-faithful claim is withdrawn. A proved def
;;    calling regex-match would therefore lose exactly the property that makes
;;    validate.llmll worth having.
;;
;;    Both reasons point the same way. The def-shell extractor in
;;    sequencer.llmll reads the artifact, runs the regexes, and reduces each
;;    check to a count or a flag. The proved defs below decide over those
;;    scalars and stay in QF-LIA. No string reaches this module.
;;
;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;; 2. WHY EACH DECISION CARRIES A CONVERSE POSTCONDITION.
;;
;;    The forward posts are eliminative one clause at a time: a body ignoring
;;    any single conjunct is refuted by the post naming it. They do not,
;;    together, forbid a body that rejects a CONFORMING artifact -- the failure
;;    mode section 7:288-291 actually describes, where a validator fitted to
;;    one run reports emptiness on the next.
;;
;;    So each decision states the converse: every conforming artifact passes.
;;    That is [V7-NO-HARDCODE]'s analogue, and it is what refutes a body
;;    comparing a row count to a literal drawn from the committed TFTP census.
;;    crux-shape-row-count-fitted.llmll is that body.
;;
;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;; 3. WHAT THIS MODULE DOES NOT DECIDE.
;;
;;    THE BARRIER CONDITION IS HERE BUT IS NOT A SHAPE CHECK, and conflating
;;    the two is the specific error this module is arranged to prevent. Five of
;;    check_dispositioned's six checks are `require` and record `failed`
;;    (:483, :486, :487, :490, :499). The sixth is `require_spec` (:493) citing
;;    driver-spec sec 6:229-231, so proposal section 3.5's rule makes it
;;    spec-defined and it records `stopped`.
;;
;;    That is why barrier-condition-met? is a SEPARATE def from
;;    dispositions-conform?. Routing it through validate.verdict-outcome would
;;    be refuted by [V7-NO-STOP], which forbids ConditionUnmet for any failing
;;    verdict -- correctly, for the three stages 4b ported, none of which has a
;;    spec-defined halt. Stage G has one. Its call site uses the sequencer's
;;    halt-with channel with the clause, not halt-verdict.
;;
;;    CONSEQUENCE FOR THE SUB-PHASE, and it corrects a settled document:
;;    proposal section 9.2 item 1 records that 4c constructs no
;;    PartialThenHalt, which holds. It does not say that 4c constructs
;;    ConditionUnmet, which it does, here. 4c therefore builds THREE of
;;    Outcome's four arms where 4b built two. [V7-ONLY-TWO] remains true as
;;    stated, being a property of verdict-outcome's codomain rather than an
;;    invariant of the port; its :source prose is scoped to 4b and is left
;;    alone rather than edited, a shipped proved module not being the place to
;;    record a later sub-phase's arithmetic.
;;
;;    check_audit (:447, stage G2) is NOT here. G2 is a gate, Phase 3 ported
;;    it, and its citation half cannot be replayed in this repository at all
;;    (harness finding F-4). No 4c stage reaches it.

(module shape

  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; Stage D: check_extraction (:386-428)
  ;;
  ;; Eight clauses over the artifact, reduced to five scalars. The four
  ;; per-row clauses (:403 object, :405 seven required keys, :407 integer line
  ;; bounds, :410 bounds in order, :422 N-prefixed rule) collapse into ONE
  ;; conforming-row count, because they are conjuncts of a single per-row
  ;; predicate and the reference halts on the first row that fails any of
  ;; them. Which clause failed changes the detail string, not the disposition,
  ;; and the detail is the shell's to build.
  ;;
  ;; rows-conforming <= normative-count is a precondition rather than a
  ;; discovered fact: the extractor counts conforming rows AMONG the normative
  ;; rows, so the subset relation holds by construction at every call site,
  ;; and it is what makes the converse post provable.
  (def extraction-conforms? [is-object: bool
                             has-normative: bool
                             has-excluded: bool
                             normative-count: int
                             rows-conforming: int] -> bool

    (pre (and (>= normative-count 0)
              (and (>= rows-conforming 0)
                   (<= rows-conforming normative-count)))
      :source "[D7-DOM] both counts are json-array lengths, so non-negative, and the conforming rows are counted among the normative rows")

    (post (=> result is-object)
      :source "[D7-OBJECT] rfc_to_implementation.py:394 - a bare array is not an extraction; the object is what carries normative and excluded")

    (post (=> result (and has-normative has-excluded))
      :source "[D7-LISTS] :397-399 - both keys are required and both must be lists; excluded is the evidence that the rubric was applied to the non-normative text rather than skipped")

    (post (=> result (> normative-count 0))
      :source "[D7-NONEMPTY] :400 - an empty normative census is a rejection, not an extraction with nothing in it")

    (post (=> result (= rows-conforming normative-count))
      :source "[D7-ROWS] :403-424 - EVERY normative row is an object carrying the seven required keys, with integer line bounds in order and a rule matching N followed by digits (N0 included; stage C authors the rule set)")

    (post (=> (and is-object
                   (and has-normative
                        (and has-excluded
                             (and (> normative-count 0)
                                  (= rows-conforming normative-count))))) result)
      :source "[D7-NO-HARDCODE] driver-spec.txt sec 7:288-291 - EVERY conforming extraction passes whatever its census size, so a validator fitted to the row count one run produced is refuted rather than merely discouraged")

    (and is-object
         (and has-normative
              (and has-excluded
                   (and (> normative-count 0)
                        (= rows-conforming normative-count))))))

  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; Stage F: the core check (:712-714)
  ;;
  ;; The reference tolerates two shapes: `core["core_ids"] if isinstance(core,
  ;; dict) else core` accepts an object carrying core_ids OR a bare array.
  ;; Harness finding F-20 measured the bare-array arm to have NO producer in
  ;; the tree -- the stub emits an object, the sole committed core.json is an
  ;; object, and the stage-F prompt template's own worked example is an object.
  ;; Proposal section 9.2 item 4 settles the port on the object shape as a
  ;; total predicate over what the reader indexes, REJECTING a bare array with
  ;; `failed` rather than accepting it silently, on 4a's corrupt-manifest
  ;; precedent.
  ;;
  ;; has-core-ids is therefore false for a bare array, and that is a
  ;; narrowing, disclosed. It is not measured against a live agent: F-20 is a
  ;; null result at n=0 live runs on whether one ever emits the bare shape.
  (def core-conforms? [has-core-ids: bool core-count: int] -> bool

    (pre (>= core-count 0)
      :source "[F7-DOM] the count is a json-array length and is never negative")

    (post (=> result has-core-ids)
      :source "[F7-KEY] :713 - the core arrives as an object carrying core_ids; the bare-array arm is narrowed away and the narrowing is disclosed (finding F-20)")

    (post (=> result (> core-count 0))
      :source "[F7-NONEMPTY] :714 - expected a non-empty core_ids list; the characteristic core is the set whose loss would mean the protocol was not implemented, and an empty one names nothing")

    (post (=> (and has-core-ids (> core-count 0)) result)
      :source "[F7-NO-HARDCODE] driver-spec.txt sec 7:288-291 - EVERY non-empty core passes whatever its length, so a validator fitted to the fifteen rows one run produced is refuted")

    (and has-core-ids (> core-count 0)))

  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; Stage G: check_dispositioned's five `failed` clauses (:483-499)
  ;;
  ;; :483 rows is a non-empty list; :486 every row has a cid; :487 every
  ;; disposition is one of the four; :490 every class matches ^C[1-6]$; :499
  ;; every row has a reason. The four per-row clauses collapse for the same
  ;; reason stage D's do.
  ;;
  ;; The SIXTH check is not here. See the header, item 3.
  (def dispositions-conform? [has-rows: bool
                              row-count: int
                              rows-conforming: int] -> bool

    (pre (and (>= row-count 0)
              (and (>= rows-conforming 0)
                   (<= rows-conforming row-count)))
      :source "[G7-DOM] both counts are json-array lengths, and the conforming rows are counted among the rows")

    (post (=> result has-rows)
      :source "[G7-ROWS-KEY] :482-483 - the dispositioned inventory arrives as an object carrying rows")

    (post (=> result (> row-count 0))
      :source "[G7-NONEMPTY] :483 - dispositions: expected rows; an empty inventory dispositions nothing")

    (post (=> result (= rows-conforming row-count))
      :source "[G7-ROWS] :486-499 - EVERY row carries a cid, a disposition from the closed four, a class matching C1 through C6, and a reason")

    (post (=> (and has-rows
                   (and (> row-count 0)
                        (= rows-conforming row-count))) result)
      :source "[G7-NO-HARDCODE] driver-spec.txt sec 7:288-291 - EVERY conforming inventory passes whatever its size, so a validator fitted to the 124 rows one run produced is refuted")

    (and has-rows
         (and (> row-count 0)
              (= rows-conforming row-count))))

  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; Stage G: check_dispositioned's ONE spec-defined clause (:493)
  ;;
  ;; require_spec, citing driver-spec sec 6:229-231. Every row dispositioned
  ;; out MUST cite a barrier from the closed list; an exclusion outside the
  ;; list is a STOP. Proposal section 3.5's rule makes this ConditionUnmet and
  ;; `stopped`, which is the disposition the reference records and the rig
  ;; already asserts (test_rfc_pipeline_integration.py:334, mode bad-barrier).
  ;;
  ;; The counts are over the DISPOSITIONED-OUT rows only, which is what makes
  ;; the subset precondition hold: a row citing a listed barrier is one of the
  ;; excluded rows.
  ;;
  ;; This is the stage-level twin of gate.gate-halts' bad-barrier parameter.
  ;; The reference enforces the condition TWICE on purpose -- stage G rejects
  ;; the disposition output and gate J re-checks a resumed run's inventory
  ;; (test_rfc_pipeline_integration.py:325-329) -- so a second call site is
  ;; faithful rather than redundant. The row-level Json predicate that feeds
  ;; both lives in spine.llmll for gate J and in sequencer.llmll for stage G;
  ;; that duplication is disclosed and retires at 4f, where the two programs
  ;; unify.
  (def barrier-condition-met? [excluded-count: int listed-count: int] -> bool

    (pre (and (>= excluded-count 0)
              (and (>= listed-count 0)
                   (<= listed-count excluded-count)))
      :source "[G6-DOM] counts over the dispositioned-out rows; a row citing a listed barrier is one of them")

    (post (= result (= excluded-count listed-count))
      :source "[G6-CLOSED] driver-spec.txt sec 6:229-231 - every dispositioned-out row MUST cite a barrier from the closed list")

    (post (=> (< listed-count excluded-count) (not result))
      :source "[G6-NOVACUOUS] a row citing an unlisted barrier MUST fail the condition, so a body answering true unconditionally is refuted rather than merely unproved")

    (= excluded-count listed-count))

  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; Sub-phase 4d, stage H: the probe manifest's shape.
  ;;
  ;; The reference checks `isinstance(probes, list) and probes` and then
  ;; indexes `p["file"]` and `p["mutant_file"]` unguarded, so a probe row
  ;; missing either key raises KeyError and tracebacks out at exit 1. The port
  ;; has no host exception to fall through, so it guards and records
  ;; failed/Errored, on the precedent proposal section 9.1 item 1 set for the
  ;; two unguarded reads in stages B and I.
  ;;
  ;; THIS IS A PRE-WRITE CHECK AND ITS HALT IS `failed`. Stage H's OTHER halt,
  ;; the probe-polarity bar in oracle.llmll, fires after feasibility.json is
  ;; written and records `stopped`. One stage, two halts, two dispositions,
  ;; and the difference is which side of the declared write they sit on.
  ;;
  ;; rows-conforming <= row-count is a precondition rather than a discovered
  ;; fact, for the same reason it is in extraction-conforms?: the conforming
  ;; rows are counted among the rows.
  (def probe-rows-conform? [is-array: bool
                            row-count: int
                            rows-conforming: int] -> bool

    (pre (and (>= row-count 0)
              (and (>= rows-conforming 0)
                   (<= rows-conforming row-count)))
      :source "[H7-DOM] both counts are json-array lengths, so non-negative, and the conforming rows are counted among the rows")

    (post (=> result is-array)
      :source "[H7-ARRAY] stage H - the reference checks isinstance(probes, list); an object here would iterate its keys and index strings")

    (post (=> result (> row-count 0))
      :source "[H7-NONEMPTY] stage H - an empty probe manifest is a rejection, since a truthiness test on the parsed list is what the reference halts on")

    (post (=> result (= rows-conforming row-count))
      :source "[H7-ROWS] stage H - EVERY row carries the file and mutant_file keys the stage indexes unguarded; a row missing either is a KeyError in the reference and a guarded failed in the port")

    (post (=> (and is-array
                   (and (> row-count 0)
                        (= rows-conforming row-count))) result)
      :source "[H7-NO-HARDCODE] driver-spec.txt sec 7:288-291 - every conforming manifest passes whatever its probe count, so a validator fitted to the count one run produced is refuted rather than discouraged")

    (and is-array
         (and (> row-count 0)
              (= rows-conforming row-count)))))
